# M1_limitation_slowmanifold_HH.ode -- where BRUTE FORCE (method M1) fails: the repelling slow manifold,
#                reduced Hodgkin-Huxley neuron (Osinga & Moehlis 2010, eq. 4.1)
#
# ======================================================================================
# WHAT TO DO IN THE XPPAUT WINDOW   (or run M1_limitation_slowmanifold_HH.xps: File > Do script)
# ======================================================================================
#  A slice of starting points at v = -55, from n = 0.3 to 0.8, crosses a thin layer just
#  below the V-nullcline, at n = 0.432617061759.  Across that layer the asymptotic phase runs
#  through (almost) every value from 0 to 1.
#   1. Viewaxes > 2D: X-axis vs, Y-axis ns, Xlabel v, Ylabel n (window as below);
#      nUmerics > Color code > Another quantity: theta, Choose, 0, 1;  Nullcline > New
#   2. Initialconds > Range over j, from 0 to 799, Steps 799, Reset storage N, Use old
#      ic's Y.  j = 0..199 is the slice from n = 0.3 to 0.8 (window 1: coloured by
#      phase; the colour jumps where the slice meets the layer, next to the V-nullcline);
#      j = 200..799 are three zooms into the layer, for windows 2-4.
#   3. Makewindow > Create; in the new window set the colour quantity again (a new window
#      copies the colour settings but not the quantity); Viewaxes > 2D: X-axis z4, Y-axis
#      theta, X from -1 to 1, Y from 0 to 1 (z4 = (n - 0.432617061759)*1e4, the position across the
#      layer magnified 10^4 times); Restore (keys r).
#   4. The same with z8 (10^8 times) and z11 (10^11 times), each in a new window.
#
#  A vertical stack of dots at the centre of windows 2 and 3: those are the runs of the
#  finer zooms (windows 3 and 4), which all lie within 1e-8 (or 1e-11) of the layer, so on
#  the coarser scale they fall at position 0, one above the other.  Ignore them there; the
#  next window spreads them out.
#
#  WHAT YOU SEE: the phase against the position across the layer.  On a scale where the
#  grid is fine enough the phase changes smoothly; here, at every zoom, neighbouring
#  starting points can still have very different phases.
#  Reduced Hodgkin-Huxley: at every zoom, down to 10^11 magnification (spacing 1e-13 in
#  n), the phase still jumps by about 0.36 between two neighbours: the transition is
#  sharper than any of these grids can resolve.
#
# WHY M1 FAILS HERE (Osinga & Moehlis, SIAM J. Appl. Dyn. Syst. 9 (2010), pp. 1223-1224):
#  "all isochrons come exponentially close to xr", the orbit along the repelling slow
#  manifold; "extreme phase sensitivity can occur on a codimension-one set in the interior
#  of B(Γ)"; "the density of the isochrons is not arbitrarily small but depends on the
#  ratio ε << 1 between the slow and fast time scales"; they call the repelling slow
#  manifold an "almost phaseless set".  M1 gives each grid point one phase, so across a
#  layer thinner than the grid spacing the isochrons merge or alias; refining the grid
#  costs N^2 integrations and cannot keep up with an exponentially thin layer.  M3
#  (continuation) follows each isochron as a curve, parameterised by arclength.
#  (The layer position here was found by zooming in with this same file; that it is the
#  repelling slow manifold is inferred from its place next to the V-nullcline, where the
#  paper puts that manifold.)
#
# THE METHOD: as M1_HH.ode (steps 1-4 there).  Extra here: n0 holds the starting n
#  exactly (copied at t = 1e-6, before the marker variables drift by eps*F), and z4, z8,
#  z11 = (n0 - lay)*10^k: XPP stores plot data in single precision, which cannot show
#  differences of 1e-10 at n = 0.433, so the zoomed windows plot these scaled offsets.
# ======================================================================================

# --- the model: the reduced Hodgkin-Huxley neuron of Osinga & Moehlis (2010), eq. (4.1):
#     m = minf(v) (fast), h = 0.8 - n;  I = 10 (repetitive firing)
am(x)=0.1*(x+40)/(1-exp(-(x+40)/10))
bm(x)=4*exp(-(x+65)/18)
an(x)=0.01*(x+55)/(1-exp(-(x+55)/10))
bn(x)=0.125*exp(-(x+65)/80)
minf(x)=am(x)/(am(x)+bm(x))
fx(x,y)=iapp-gna*minf(x)^3*(0.8-y)*(x-ena)-gk*y^4*(x-ek)-gl*(x-el)
fy(x,y)=an(x)*(1-y)-bn(x)*y
par iapp=10,gna=120,gk=36,gl=0.3,ena=50,ek=-77,el=-54.4

# --- the ingredients (see M1_HH.ode)
par per=11.84628522, thr=-20, tc0=11.74002818

# --- step 1: the starting points.  One Range over j = 0..799 covers all four windows:
#     j = 0..199 the whole slice from n = ylo to yhi; j = 200..399, 400..599, 600..799 the
#     layer lay +- 1e-4, +- 1e-8, +- 1e-11.  At t = 1e-6 an event puts the starting point
#     y0 = ystart(j) (computed in double precision) into the trajectory (V, N), the
#     marker (vs, ns) that window 1 plots, and n0.  (One Range keeps all the runs in XPP's
#     storage, so every window can redraw them; a second Range would replace them.)
par j=0, vlay=-55, ylo=0.3, yhi=0.8, lay=0.432617061759
hw(k)=if(k<1.5)then(1e-4)else(if(k<2.5)then(1e-8)else(1e-11))
ystart(i)=if(i<199.5)then(ylo+(yhi-ylo)*i/199)else(lay+hw(flr(i/200))*(2*(i-200*flr(i/200))/199-1))
par eps=1e-9
vs'=eps*fx(vs,ns)
ns'=eps*fy(vs,ns)
n0'=0
init vs=-55,ns=0.432617061759,n0=0.432617061759
V'=fx(V,N)
N'=fy(V,N)
init V=-55,N=0.432617061759
global 1 t-1e-6 {V=vlay;N=ystart(j);vs=vlay;ns=ystart(j);n0=ystart(j)}

# --- steps 2 and 3: the asymptotic phase at every upward crossing of v = thr
theta'=0
init theta=-1
global 1 V-thr {theta=mod((tc0-t)/per,1)}

# --- the position across the layer, magnified (lay = the layer, found by zooming in)
aux z4=(n0-lay)*1e4
aux z8=(n0-lay)*1e8
aux z11=(n0-lay)*1e11

#     (bound 1e12: XPP stops a run when any column, aux included, passes it; z11 is large
#      for starting points far from the layer)
@ meth=rk4, dt=0.01, trans=71.0777, total=86.4779, nout=100, bound=1e12, maxstor=1000000
@ rangereset=no, rangeoldic=yes
@ xp=vs, yp=ns, xlo=-90, xhi=52, ylo=0.3, yhi=0.8, lt=-3, colormap=1, nmesh=200
done
